Title ' 9-BIT EVEN/ODD PARITY GENERATOR/CHECKER'
Designer ' SCOTT LIEN'
Date ' 5-29-91'

Description

  This application uses the PEEL22CV10A as a 9-bit even/odd parity
  generator/checker. The table below describes the operation:

  ---------------------------------------------------
  | Number of high | Parity_I | Even_Odd | Parity_O |
  | inputs A - H   |          |          |          |
  ---------------------------------------------------
  |    Even        |     1    |    0     |     H    |
  ---------------------------------------------------
  |    Odd         |     1    |    0     |     L    |
  ---------------------------------------------------
  |    Even        |     0    |    0     |     L    |
  ---------------------------------------------------
  |    Odd         |     0    |    0     |     H    |
  ---------------------------------------------------
  |    Even        |     1    |    1     |     L    |
  ---------------------------------------------------
  |    Odd         |     1    |    1     |     H    |
  ---------------------------------------------------
  |    Even        |     0    |    1     |     H    |
  ---------------------------------------------------
  |    Odd         |     0    |    1     |     L    |
  ---------------------------------------------------

  It is very easy to use the exclusive-OR operations to implement a parity
  function. To build a N-bit parity generator/checker, (N-1) 2-input
  exclusive-OR gates are required. The same function can be implemented by
  the AND-OR logic but it requires 2 to the (N-1)th power product terms. For
  a 9-bit parity function, 256 product terms are needed. Even though the
  PEEL22CV10A device does not have this many product terms, this function can
  still be implemented in the device. The solution is to use two macro cells
  to implement the two 4-bit parity generators. Then, both the outputs are
  fed back and XORed with the 9th bit (Parity_In). The penalty with this
  method is the added delay caused by the feedback of the 4-bit parity
  generator outputs. However, if this function is implemented in the
  PEEL22CV10A device, the delay would be 2 to 3 ns less than if it was
  implemented in the industry standard 22V10 device. This is because the
  outputs in the 22V10 device is fed back from the pin, whereas in the
  PEEL22CV10A the outputs can be configured to feedback directly from the
  OR gate.

End_Desc;

PEEL22CV10A+

A PIN 1

"I/O CONFIGURATION DECLARATION
"IOC (PIN_NO 'PIN_NAME' OUTPUT_POLARITY OUTPUT_TYPE FEEDBACK_TYPE)
B pin 2
C pin 3
D pin 4
E pin 5
F pin 6 
G pin 7 
H pin 8
PARITY_I PIN 9
EVEN_ODD PIN 10
IOC (14 '' POS COM FEED_PIN)
IOC (15 '' POS COM FEED_PIN)
IOC (16 '' Pos Com Feed_Pin)
IOC (17 '' Pos Com Feed_Pin)
IOC (18 '' Pos Com Feed_Pin)
IOC (19 '' Pos Com Feed_Pin)
IOC (20 '' POS COM FEED_PIN)
IOC (21 'E_H' Pos In Feed_Or)
IOC (22 'A_D' Pos In Feed_Or)
IOC (23 'PARITY_O' Pos OutCom Feed_Pin)

AR NODE 25  "Global Asynchronous Reset
SP NODE 26  "Global Synchronous Preset


DEFINE


EQUATIONS

AR = 0;

SP = 0;

PARITY_O.And = 0;
PARITY_O.Com = (A_D $ E_H $ PARITY_I) & !EVEN_ODD #
               !(A_D $ E_H $ PARITY_I) & EVEN_ODD;

A_D.And = 0;
A_D.Com = A $ B $ C $ D;

E_H.And = 0;
E_H.Com = E $ F $ G $ H;
